From 036c663679471da1adba6efe4f6471ef1a36170c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicole@muzz.in>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 07:11:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] script: precise :has() invalidation through stylo's relative
 selector invalidator

A :has() anchor used to restyle its whole subtree whenever anything below
it changed. On ynet.co.il `html` anchors `html:has(.bepo-audio-header)
.grecaptcha-badge`, so nearly every DOM change restyled all ~4000
elements and each forced reflow cost 30-40 ms.

stylo already builds the relative selector invalidation map (the classes,
ids, attributes, element names, states and tree-structural pseudo-classes
inside :has() arguments) and has Gecko's RelativeSelectorInvalidator,
which is generic over TElement. Drive it from script the way Gecko's
RestyleManager drives it through geckolib's glue: after a state change,
after an attribute change, after an element is inserted, before one is
removed, and after a child-list change for :empty / :nth-child /
:first-child inside :has(). Only a change that matches a feature in some
:has() argument walks to the flagged anchors, and the invalidator then
restyles just the elements whose selectors depend on the anchor. Script
reads the stylist through a new Layout::stylist(), and the anchors the
invalidator dirtied are noted in the document's dirty-root bookkeeping
afterwards.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
